From: (local patch, not an upstream commit) QM1 Debian Maintainers <qm1-debian@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] wifi: rtw88: sdio: copy RX status after updating the frequency

Carried from the upstream rtw-next commit

  c76f73c36343f041adee1d0875fc7f3597d00443
  "wifi: rtw88: sdio: copy RX status after updating the frequency"
  (Hungyu Lin, 2026-10-09)

which is *not* part of the upstream snapshot this module is built from
(05441d697f7f9a927264f4058cbbc6a03dfcac9d, a torvalds/linux master
snapshot of 2026-11-03): master only merged rtw-next up to the
"rtw-next-2026-10-08" tag, so every 8723B fix from 2026-10-09 onwards
is missing from the tree we ship.

Why we carry it:

  rtw_sdio_rx_skb() copies rx_status into the skb *before* it calls
  rtw_update_rx_freq_for_invalid().  When the frequency is corrected,
  the skb still carries the stale value when mac80211 sees the frame.
  The PCI and USB RX paths already copy the status after the update.

  The QM1's RTL8723BS is SDIO, so this is the path it actually takes:
  the frame frequency that mac80211 gets to see is the wrong one, and
  mac80211 rejects a beacon whose frequency does not match the channel
  it expects (ieee80211_rx_beacon_freq_valid()).  The symptom is
  "beacon loss" on a link that is otherwise up - one of the ways the
  QM1's Wi-Fi used to drop off.

  The change moves three lines; the PCI and USB paths are unaffected.
---
diff --git a/sdio.c b/sdio.c
index dc2fd0f8f9ff..7c22f0ba5329 100644
--- a/sdio.c
+++ b/sdio.c
@@ -1332,8 +1332,6 @@ static void rtw_sdio_rx_skb(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev, struct sk_buff *skb,
 			    u32 pkt_offset, struct rtw_rx_pkt_stat *pkt_stat,
 			    struct ieee80211_rx_status *rx_status)
 {
-	*IEEE80211_SKB_RXCB(skb) = *rx_status;
-
 	if (pkt_stat->is_c2h) {
 		skb_put(skb, pkt_stat->pkt_len + pkt_offset);
 		rtw_fw_c2h_cmd_rx_irqsafe(rtwdev, pkt_offset, skb);
@@ -1346,6 +1344,7 @@ static void rtw_sdio_rx_skb(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev, struct sk_buff *skb,
 	rtw_update_rx_freq_for_invalid(rtwdev, skb, rx_status, pkt_stat);
 	rtw_rx_stats(rtwdev, pkt_stat->vif, skb);
 
+	*IEEE80211_SKB_RXCB(skb) = *rx_status;
 	ieee80211_rx_irqsafe(rtwdev->hw, skb);
 }
 
